{"summary":"This PR adds an AnnouncementCard component that renders employee-authored HTML using dangerouslySetInnerHTML without sanitization, creating an XSS vulnerability.","rating":"major-issues","readiness":"changes-required","strengths":["Clean component structure and clear TypeScript props typing."],"assumptions":["The announcements API returns raw HTML strings authored by employees, which may contain malicious payloads if not sanitized."],"findings":[{"file":"src/features/announcements/AnnouncementCard.tsx","line":8,"endLine":8,"evidence":"      <div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: bodyHtml }} />","title":"Unsanitized HTML rendered via dangerouslySetInnerHTML","why":"The bodyHtml prop comes from an API and is written by employees. Directly rendering it without client-side or server-side sanitization exposes users to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks if an employee account is compromised or malicious input is submitted.","fix":"Sanitize the HTML string using a robust library like DOMPurify before passing it to dangerouslySetInnerHTML.","suggestedCode":"import DOMPurify from 'dompurify';

export function AnnouncementCard({ title, bodyHtml }: Props) {
  const sanitizedHtml = DOMPurify.sanitize(bodyHtml);
  return (
    <article>
      <h2>{title}</h2>
      <div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: sanitizedHtml }} />
    </article>
  );
}","category":"security","kind":"bug","severity":"critical","confidence":0.95}]}

{"summary":"This pull request introduces an AnnouncementCard component that renders raw HTML content from an API using dangerouslySetInnerHTML, posing an XSS vulnerability if employee-authored content is not sanitized.","rating":"needs-improvement","readiness":"changes-required","strengths":["Clean component structure with appropriate TypeScript props typing."],"assumptions":["The announcements API returns content that might include HTML written by various employees, making it a vector for stored XSS."],"findings":[{"file":"src/features/announcements/AnnouncementCard.tsx","line":8,"endLine":8,"evidence":"     <div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: bodyHtml }} />","title":"Unsanitized HTML rendered via dangerouslySetInnerHTML","why":"Since bodyHtml is written by employees, rendering it directly without sanitization allows potential stored XSS attacks if an employee account is compromised or malicious input is injected.","fix":"Sanitize the HTML string using a robust library like DOMPurify before passing it to dangerouslySetInnerHTML.","suggestedCode":"import DOMPurify from 'dompurify';

export function AnnouncementCard({ title, bodyHtml }: Props) {
  const sanitizedHtml = DOMPurify.sanitize(bodyHtml);
  return (
    <article>
      <h2>{title}</h2>
      <div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: sanitizedHtml }} />
    </article>
  );
}","category":"security","kind":"bug","severity":"critical","confidence":0.95}]}

{"summary":"This PR introduces an AchievementsPage component that fetches data using RTK Query and duplicates it into the Redux store via a useEffect hook. This pattern introduces redundant state, potential sync delays, and unnecessary re-renders.","rating":"needs-improvement","readiness":"changes-required","strengths":["Uses typed RTK Query hooks for data fetching.","Clean component structure separating the list presentation."],"assumptions":["The achievementsSlice exists and has a setAchievements action and selectAchievements selector.","RTK Query is the standard server-state management approach for this project."],"findings":[{"file":"src/features/achievements/AchievementsPage.tsx","line":12,"endLine":15,"evidence":"  useEffect(() => {","title":"Avoid copying server state into Redux store","why":"Using an effect to sync RTK Query data into Redux state duplicates server state, causes extra renders, and breaks the single source of truth pattern provided by RTK Query caches.","fix":"Remove the redundant Redux state slice for achievements and consume data directly from the RTK Query hook, or use selector transformations if state needs formatting.","suggestedCode":"export function AchievementsPage() {
  const { data: achievements = [] } = useGetAchievementsQuery();
  return <AchievementList items={achievements} />;
}","category":"state-management","kind":"bug","severity":"medium","confidence":0.95}]}

{"summary":"The PR introduces an AchievementsPage that fetches data via RTK Query and synchronizes it into Redux state using an effect. This duplicates server state into client state and introduces an unnecessary synchronization step.","rating":"needs-improvement","readiness":"changes-required","strengths":["Clean and readable component structure."],"assumptions":["The achievements slice and selectAchievements selector are defined correctly elsewhere."],"findings":[{"file":"src/features/achievements/AchievementsPage.tsx","line":10,"endLine":10,"evidence":"  const achievements = useSelector(selectAchievements);","title":"Duplicating RTK Query server state into Redux store","why":"RTK Query already caches server state globally. Copying the result into local Redux state via an effect creates redundant state, adds extra boilerplate, and can lead to stale UI states or out-of-sync data.","fix":"Remove the achievements slice and useSelector/useDispatch synchronization. Consume the data directly from useGetAchievementsQuery.","suggestedCode":"export function AchievementsPage() {
  const { data: achievements = [] } = useGetAchievementsQuery();
  return <AchievementList items={achievements} />;
}","category":"state-management","kind":"improvement","severity":"medium","confidence":0.95}]}

{"summary":"This pull request introduces a custom hook to track the window width. However, it fails to clean up the resize event listener, leading to a memory leak.","rating":"needs-improvement","readiness":"changes-required","strengths":["Clean and concise hook implementation structure."],"assumptions":["No PR description or context was provided, so we assume this hook is intended to be used across various components requiring responsive window measurements."],"findings":[{"file":"src/hooks/useWindowWidth.ts","line":9,"endLine":9,"evidence":"  }, []);","title":"Missing event listener cleanup","why":"The useEffect adds a resize event listener to the window but fails to return a cleanup function to remove it. When components using this hook mount and unmount, listeners accumulate, causing a memory leak.","fix":"Return a cleanup function from the effect that calls window.removeEventListener.","suggestedCode":"  useEffect(() => {
    const onResize = () => setWidth(window.innerWidth);
    window.addEventListener("resize", onResize);
    return () => window.removeEventListener("resize", onResize);
  }, []);","category":"correctness","kind":"bug","severity":"high","confidence":1}]}

{"summary":"This pull request introduces a LeaveRequestForm component to handle leave submissions. However, it lacks error handling, loading states during submission, and proper state management integration.","rating":"needs-improvement","readiness":"changes-required","strengths":["Used appropriate semantic HTML elements like label and textarea with matching IDs.","Provided a status role for the submission message for better accessibility."],"assumptions":["The submitLeaveRequest function can throw an error if the network request fails.","The project uses RTK Query as mentioned in the project context, but this component calls an independent API utility function directly."],"findings":[{"file":"src/features/leave/LeaveRequestForm.tsx","line":10,"endLine":10,"evidence":"await submitLeaveRequest({ reason });","title":"Missing error handling for leave request submission","why":"If submitLeaveRequest rejects due to network failure or validation error, the promise will throw an unhandled rejection, leaving the user without any feedback and the form stuck.","fix":"Wrap the submission call in a try/catch block and set an error message state to inform the user.","suggestedCode":"try {
  await submitLeaveRequest({ reason });
  setMessage("Leave request sent.");
} catch {
  setMessage("Failed to send leave request. Please try again.");
}","category":"api","kind":"bug","severity":"high","confidence":0.95},{"file":"src/features/leave/LeaveRequestForm.tsx","line":8,"endLine":12,"evidence":"async function handleSubmit(e: FormEvent) {","title":"Missing loading state during form submission","why":"The submit button remains clickable during the async request, allowing the user to submit multiple requests concurrently.","fix":"Introduce an isSubmitting boolean state to disable the submit button and textarea while the request is in flight.","category":"correctness","kind":"risk","severity":"medium","confidence":0.9}]}

{"summary":"The PR introduces a SaveEntryButton component using an RTK Query mutation to update a timesheet entry. It has a correctness flaw when the mutation fails, as saved state incorrectly displays anyway, and lacks error handling or loading state feedback on the button.","rating":"needs-improvement","readiness":"changes-required","strengths":["Uses accessible status role for the saved confirmation message.","Correctly disables the button during mutation loading state."],"assumptions":["updateEntry mutation returns a result object that can be checked for errors."],"findings":[{"file":"src/features/timesheet/SaveEntryButton.tsx","line":10,"endLine":12,"evidence":"await updateEntry(entry);","title":"Unhandled mutation error and false positive success state","why":"If updateEntry fails or is rejected, setSaved(true) still executes, displaying a 'Saved' confirmation even though the save failed. Furthermore, unhandled promise rejections can occur if RTK Query throws on error rather than returning an error object.","fix":"Check the result of updateEntry and only set saved to true if the mutation was successful.","suggestedCode":"const result = await updateEntry(entry);
if (!('error' in result)) {
  setSaved(true);
}","category":"api","kind":"bug","severity":"high","confidence":0.95}]}

{"summary":"This pull request introduces a SaveEntryButton component that triggers an RTK Query mutation and displays a temporary success message. However, it lacks error handling and confirmation of mutation success.","rating":"needs-improvement","readiness":"ready-with-minor-changes","strengths":["Uses semantic ARIA role 'status' for success feedback","Disables the button while the mutation is loading to prevent duplicate requests"],"assumptions":["The mutation returns a result object or throws on error that should be handled."],"findings":[{"file":"src/features/timesheet/SaveEntryButton.tsx","line":10,"endLine":12,"evidence":"await updateEntry(entry);","title":"Unhandled mutation error and response status","why":"If the updateEntry mutation fails, the code still proceeds to set saved to true, misleading the user into thinking their changes were persisted successfully.","fix":"Check the result of updateEntry or unwrap the promise to handle errors and confirm success before setting saved to true.","suggestedCode":"try {
      await updateEntry(entry).unwrap();
      setSaved(true);
    } catch {
      // handle error
    }","category":"api","kind":"bug","severity":"high","confidence":0.95}]}

{"summary":"This pull request introduces an ApprovalStatus type and a statusLabel helper function. However, the helper function lacks a return statement or default case for the pending status, leading to a runtime return of undefined.","rating":"needs-improvement","readiness":"changes-required","strengths":["Clean and concise implementation of a status helper utility."],"assumptions":["The statusLabel function is expected to return a string label for all valid ApprovalStatus values, including 'pending'."],"findings":[{"file":"src/features/achievements/statusLabel.ts","line":3,"endLine":10,"evidence":"export function statusLabel(status: ApprovalStatus) {","title":"Missing case for pending status in statusLabel function","why":"The switch statement handles 'approved' and 'rejected' but omits 'pending'. When called with a pending status, the function returns undefined implicitly, which can cause UI bugs where labels fail to render.","fix":"Add a case for 'pending' or a default case to ensure a string is always returned.","suggestedCode":"export function statusLabel(status: ApprovalStatus) {
  switch (status) {
    case "approved":
      return "Approved";
    case "rejected":
      return "Rejected";
    case "pending":
      return "Pending";
  }
}","category":"correctness","kind":"bug","severity":"high","confidence":0.95}]}

{"summary":"The PR introduces a new RTK Query API slice for the timesheet feature with endpoints for fetching and adding entries. However, the mutation endpoint lacks cache tag invalidation, which will prevent the entry list from updating automatically after a new entry is added.","rating":"good","readiness":"ready-with-minor-changes","strengths":["Clean setup of an RTK Query slice following standard Redux Toolkit conventions.","Appropriate typing for query and mutation arguments and return types."],"assumptions":["The backend returns the newly created Entry object on a successful POST to /entries."],"findings":[{"file":"src/features/timesheet/timesheetApi.ts","line":13,"endLine":15,"evidence":"    addEntry: build.mutation<Entry, NewEntry>({","title":"Missing invalidatesTags on addEntry mutation","why":"Without invalidating the 'Entry' tag, the cached result of getEntries will not refetch when a new entry is successfully posted, leaving the UI out of sync with the server until a manual page reload.","fix":"Add invalidatesTags: ['Entry'] to the addEntry mutation definition so the cache correctly invalidates and refetches upon mutation success.","suggestedCode":"    addEntry: build.mutation<Entry, NewEntry>({
      query: (body) => ({ url: "/entries", method: "POST", body }),
      invalidatesTags: ["Entry"],
    }),","category":"api","kind":"bug","severity":"high","confidence":0.95}]}

